  # Self-improving agents: what changes, what persists, and how to prove it
## **How to prove a self-improving agent actually improved**
![Prove a candidate with target cases, repeated runs, held-out cases, and regression checks.](https://arize.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/fig-proof-gates.png)Each evaluation gate answers a different failure mode.

Every candidate needs an evidence plan, not one universal checklist. [Start with repeat comparisons](https://arize.com/ai-agents/agent-evaluation/) and cases outside the update data; then add regression, constraint, and independent-review checks as the change becomes broader or harder to reverse.

### **Discovery and evaluation solve different problems**
A team can write deterministic checks for malformed tool arguments, an LLM judge for a known semantic criterion, or a regression test for a failure it has already seen.

### **What evaluations have asked over time**
- **Broader validation (Patel et al., 2024; Ye et al., 2026)**: [Large Language Models Can Self-Improve At Web Agent Tasks](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20309) added measures of performance, robustness, capabilities, and trajectory quality on WebArena; [On the Fragility of Self-Improving Agents](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.18066) used repeated runs and shuffled task order to re-evaluate memory-based methods.

## **Autonomy should depend on reversibility and blast radius**
### **Minimum promotion evidence by risk**
- Memory, prompt, or guidance: snapshot the candidate; repeat comparisons on held-out tasks; check regressions; name the rollback path.

## **Measure the whole agent**
### **Test the candidate as a system, not only as a prompt**
A code change can produce a different trajectory even when the model and prompt stay constant.

Validation should therefore execute the **complete candidate agent** whenever possible.

This is stronger evidence than scoring a proposed prompt or diff in isolation because it tests the behavior that users will actually inherit.

This provides stronger evidence than reviewing a proposed prompt or code change in isolation because the candidate is tested as the system users will actually experience.

## **FAQ**
### **How do you evaluate a self-improving agent?**
- Held-out cases: does the gain transfer beyond the data used to create or select the update?
- Regression and constraint checks: did the agent preserve prior successes and stay within relevant safety, permission, cost, or latency limits?

